{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/unchanged-demo-trigger-check.md", "content": "# Unchanged demos and trigger generalization

An input-output pair with identical grids still carries information: the observed marker/object configuration did not activate a transformation. Log it alongside transformed examples, including its palette, geometry, marker colors, and surrounding background. Use it to distinguish broad rules (e.g. every sparse shape triggers extension) from conditional rules (only particular marker configurations or orientations do).

Before predicting an unchanged test grid, explicitly compare its structural features with all demonstrated positive and negative cases. If the demos establish a transformation for one motif but the test has a novel configuration, do not treat identity as a low-risk default; seek a discriminating demo when budget permits, or submit only a materially supported rule. Every demo response, including unchanged outputs, must update the trigger/scope ledger before another request or submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/unchanged-demo-trigger-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/trajectory-after-error.md", "content": "# Revising sparse trajectory hypotheses after a wrong output

A wrong submission that extends only one apparent ray is not repaired by shifting its start or changing a single cell. Re-derive the generator from each demo by recording every seed marker, color, direction vector, and endpoint. In the example reviewed here, one demo was identity (a sparse object did not trigger extension), while the larger demo showed simultaneous rays: a 7-ray extended down-right from its marker, and 6-ray(s) extended along the other diagonal direction from multiple marker locations. The test had several 9 markers plus paired 7 and 6 markers; assuming the salient 9-marker cluster was irrelevant and extending just one 6 ray / one short 7 ray was unsupported and was rejected. General lesson: compare the full input geometry to positive and negative demos; infer whether multiple marker colors or every compatible seed launches a ray, and project each ray through the full grid to the evidenced boundary. Preserve original objects and background. Do not submit a one-color or one-ray interpretation unless it accounts for all trajectory behavior in the demonstrations.

This example is not a universal rule for launching rays from all markers: use it to formulate competing hypotheses, then select the one that explains the complete demos and test configuration.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/trajectory-after-error.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/post-error-evidence-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Post-error evidence checkpoint

After an incorrect submission, treat the feedback as falsifying the submitted candidate as a whole. Re-read all input and demos and identify which assumptions may be wrong: output scope/dimensions, panel pairing, value mapping, trigger condition, or exceptional cells. Do not respond with cosmetic variants (for example, switching between a uniform background and that background plus one guessed marker) unless a complete demo-validated rule supports the change.

For divider-separated panels, explicitly test aligned cell-pair mappings across every demo coordinate and account for all output values, including rare exceptions. If the mapping or operation is still ambiguous, request a new demo chosen to distinguish named alternatives; do not spend another submission on an ungrounded guess. Validate the transferred test output's dimensions and all cells before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/post-error-evidence-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/repeated-pattern-misdirection.md", "content": "# Periodic output versus input-driven transformation

When several examples show periodic full-grid outputs, first verify whether the output is generated solely from a fixed palette cycle or whether it is a transformation of the input's marker arrangement. Record the complete mapping from each input row/cell to the corresponding output, including locations where the input is blank and output is nonblank. A small demo with distinctive sparse markers can reveal an operation such as filling a sequence, shifting or cycling colors, or extending a pattern; do not ignore it because other examples are dense stripes.

After a periodic-pattern submission fails, reject that generator as a whole unless it can be reconciled with every demo. Do not spend further submissions on arbitrary phase or palette variants. Use remaining demos to resolve explicit alternatives (e.g. row-wise versus column-wise sequence, marker-order preservation versus fixed cycle), then validate the candidate on all known examples before resubmitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/repeated-pattern-misdirection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/post-response-demo-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Post-response demo checkpoint

After every requested demo returns, stop and integrate it before choosing another action. Add its dimensions, separators/panels, marker locations, and output mapping to the ledger; then replay candidate rules against all demos received so far. State internally what new evidence the demo adds and whether it eliminates any hypothesis. Do not request the next demo simply because uncertainty remains: name at least two live candidates and the observable distinction a further example could resolve. This checkpoint is especially important after a sequence of demo requests, where the cost accumulates while no test prediction is being validated.

If the accumulated examples do not establish a unique rule, request further evidence only when the potential discriminator has concrete value. Otherwise use the available evidence to form and verify the best supported prediction, rather than allowing demo gathering to become automatic.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/post-response-demo-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/cross-demo-count-palette.md", "content": "# Cross-demo count rules with palette changes

For divider-separated compartments, record each panel's anomaly-marker count, output cell, and input background/output palette. Separate the structural rule from palette choice: for example, an exact count may produce an accent, while other counts preserve the demo's background. Demonstrations may use different background numbers, so avoid hardcoding a numeric mapping learned from only one pair.

After an incorrect prediction, audit the full count-to-output table across every demo before attempting a correction. Explicitly mark unobserved counts (especially zero) as unknown rather than assigning them a guessed value. Recompute panel boundaries and counts from the input, then verify the predicted output shape and every output cell.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/cross-demo-count-palette.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-integration", "file": "references/demo-chain-pitfall.md", "content": "# Demo-chain pitfall: integrate before spending again

A run of demonstrations can be costly without improving the prediction if each new pair is accepted and another request follows without analysis. After each response, record its dimensions, structural lattice/period, output shape, and exact correspondence; then test candidate mappings against all examples received so far. Dissimilar-looking examples may still reveal a common abstraction, so do not treat unfamiliar appearance as a reason to request yet another example.

Before each request, state internally at least two competing rules and the concrete observation expected to distinguish them. If the test rule remains uncertain but the demos already impose no further useful distinction, make a validated prediction from the evidence or acknowledge uncertainty through the protocol's allowed actions; do not reflexively consume the demo cap. A wrong answer should trigger a material reconsideration of scope, output dimensions, or mapping—not a cosmetic variant.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-integration/references/demo-chain-pitfall.md"}
